\section{What the page opens on}{

Every biomarker the Biomarker control offers, at one visit, \code{visit} (the
first visit when none is named), with one value type, \code{value_type}. A
difference (\code{comparison = "difference"}, the default) compares two groups of
one column, \code{group_by} and \code{levels} (the first column with two groups or
more, and its first two groups, when none are named); each row is the
standardised difference (Hedges' g) with Welch's p-value. A correlation
(\code{comparison = "correlation"}) correlates every biomarker with one variable,
\code{with}, a biomarker at a visit or a participant-level number, by \code{method};
the biomarker that is that variable is not a row of its own. A hazard ratio
(\code{comparison = "hazard"}), offered only with an outcomes table, cuts every
biomarker at its median and compares high against low on one endpoint,
\code{endpoint} (the first when none is named); each row is the hazard ratio,
High over Low, with the log-rank p-value. The outcomes table is read as
\code{\link[=Widget_StratifiedSurvival]{Widget_StratifiedSurvival()}} reads it, by the same settings. The p-values
are adjusted across the rows by \code{adjustment}, Benjamini-Hochberg by default.
}

\section{Statistics shipped with the page}{

The chart computes no estimate, interval or p-value. It asks R once for the
whole screen, on a frame with one row per participant and one column per
biomarker, keeping a participant who has only some of the biomarkers, so that
each row is of the participants who have its value. The widget stores R's
answer for the screen the settings open on.

A click on a row opens that biomarker's chart, which asks R for its own
statistics. For a difference it is the group comparison, on the biomarker,
at the screen's one visit, with only the two groups and Welch's test; for a
correlation it is the association scatter, with the biomarker along the
bottom and the variable up the side, by the same method; for a hazard ratio
it is the stratified survival chart, on the biomarker cut at its median, on
the same endpoint. For every row the widget stores exactly what that chart
asks when it opens, under the screen's filters. On the synthetic study, a
difference between the arms is one screen and twelve group comparisons.

A reader who moves a control of the screen, or of the chart a row opens, to
a view that was not computed is told that statistics are unavailable for
it: the screen's rows stay empty, and the chart prints the same. The page
never shows one view's numbers under another.

The page states which R computed the results, and the widget names the
baseline visits to the chart outright, as \code{\link[=Widget_GroupComparison]{Widget_GroupComparison()}} does.
}

\section{Filters}{

With a participant table the chart has filters, set by the setting
\code{filters} under safety.viz's rules: a filter opens on its \code{start} when the
data has it and otherwise on All, a filter set \code{all = FALSE} has no All and
opens on its first value, and \code{multiple = TRUE} lets several values through.
R works out what each filter opens on as the chart does, and stores the
results for those participants.

The first value of an \code{all = FALSE} filter is the one exception, because
the chart lists a filter's values in the order of the reader's browser,
which R cannot know: for a letter with an accent or for punctuation it can
differ from R's order, by code point. So the widget hands the chart R's
first value as the filter's \code{start}, and the page opens on the participants
R computed for, though that value may not be the first in the list.
}

\section{Bundles}{

The widget loads bio.viz's bundle and the copy of safety.viz's bundle that
bio.viz itself builds its chart from. Both are copied from bio.viz, with the
bio.viz commit and a checksum per file recorded beside them in
\code{system.file("htmlwidgets", "lib", "SOURCE.json", package = "gsm.bio")}. They
are bio.viz v0.2.0 and safety.viz v1.9.0, the first safety.viz with the kit
the chart is built from, as bio.viz takes it from safety.viz's \code{dev} branch;
the record says from which commit. gsm.safety carries an earlier safety.viz
without the kit, and once it carries v1.9.0 the widgets can take the bundle
from there.
}

